  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the graph of $y={2}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/4fa685525f4f08d801ff25bd0d7dc2ed94ca35b54424531a5a7d5568562234c4.png)\n\n- To plot $y={2}^{x-3},$ we shift the graph of $y={2}^{x}$ horizontally by $3$ units to the right. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(3,1),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/3c39774191845f83c6bfc1462771b9122ecde89ae0b33b550603d1d5007095db.png)\n\n- To plot ${2}^{x-3}-2,$ we shift the graph of $y={2}^{x-3}$ vertically by $2$ units down. The point $(3,1)$ moves to $(3,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/ff047be1b3289b16e4c64bafe256e42b1deebdd07c24694d0e5bcaba81710826.png)",
